WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.390
- An employee of EY, formerly
known as Ernst &amp; Young,

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.820
has been found dead in the office

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.490
in Australia after work event.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.560
This is what I want to talk about today.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.090
So welcome to a new coffee break,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.890
here on my channel, Firm Learning,

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.070
the place where I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.320
in the first years of your career.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.390
The death of this EY employee is

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.950
what everyone has been talking about

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.860
in the consulting community
in the last one or two weeks.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:26.693
And in this video,

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.620
I wanna first discuss the circumstances

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.420
that are known in this specific case,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.790
but then, second, in a more general way,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.100
talk about the work-life balance,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.830
the working hours that
you can currently expect

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.000
to find in consulting.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.010
I will, third, talk about travel,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.870
how much travel will be expected

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.690
from you in consulting
at this day and age.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.300
And then, fourth, I will
make some conclusions,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.950
what, in my humble opinion,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.040
this means for the attractiveness
of a consulting career,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.130
and also, how this changed
compared to the pre-corona time.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.540
Let's start with the first topic,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.120
the death of the consultant in Australia.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.290
And also to make this clear,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.170
I by no means want to imply
that the working conditions

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.800
at EY were actually
responsible for the death.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.290
This is not what I want
to say with this video.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.810
But if you do read the
Associated News reports,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.610
there do seem to be some signs

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.520
that the lady involved
was indeed overworked

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.580
in the last weeks, it was busy season.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.890
And if you read, read it in other places,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.720
there were other consultants saying that,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.310
especially in audit,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.520
the sector where this lady
worked from July to September,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.490
so the busy season where
many of the big corporates

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.890
need to complete and finalize their books,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.990
the working hours can be crushing.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.990
Some people talked about 70, 80 hours,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.577
plus the work expected from you.

00:01:41.577 --> 00:01:44.250
And the Big Four employees
from that region reported

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.030
that working in these firms
felt like running an Ironman,

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.310
being part of an Ironman contest,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.590
where allegedly, some
seniors would tell you

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.210
that you should, quote/unquote,
"Either survive or leave."

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.857
And it was this mindset that
people were saying that,

00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:01.957
"Well, if I needed to work like this,

00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:03.547
"if I did it in the past,

00:02:03.547 --> 00:02:06.457
"then it truly must be
fair for you as the junior

00:02:06.457 --> 00:02:08.211
"to now go through the same thing,

00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:10.590
"live through the same hours as well."

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.360
And in the specific case at EY,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.640
there also seemed to be
some other circumstances

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.473
adding on it.

00:02:15.473 --> 00:02:17.070
Apparently this lady was from India

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.170
on a work visa in Australia.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.870
This can put additional pressure on people

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.870
because your visa is
usually directly attached

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.310
to your employment.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.340
And if you do not manage
to find another employer

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.070
who sponsors your visa,
if you quit your job,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.770
you do not meet your
visa conditions anymore,

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.320
which might even lead to your deportation.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.190
So to some extent,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.280
these people seem to be locked
in into their situation.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.210
And allegedly,

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.180
some of the firms are also
taking advantage of that

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.620
by not promoting these people

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.780
and leaving them on junior positions

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.420
because they know that
they cannot really leave.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.040
Now, as many things are still unknown

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.600
about this specific case,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.310
I want to rather abstract the topic

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.460
to a more general discussion
about working hours

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.190
and work-life balance in consulting.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.260
And I will take this
from two different sides.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.800
First, I will talk about
my own humble experiences

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.460
having worked at an MBB strategy
consulting firm in Germany.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.270
And then, I also did surveys
on LinkedIn, on Instagram,

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.310
collecting many of your inputs,

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.200
also considering the current situation

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.380
which is surely different
today after COVID.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.900
All of this with the goal
to hopefully help you make

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.460
more informed decisions

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.380
whether you want to
join consulting or not,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.320
so that everyone knows what
he or she is getting into.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.870
Let me start by talking about what I know

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.140
the reality has been if you
would've worked in a company

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.670
like BCG, Bain, or McKinsey
up until the pandemic.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.770
So typical work week looked like that,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.130
that on Monday morning, you
would fly into the client,

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.980
of needing to wake up at four or 5:00 a.m.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:50.813
because of course,

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:52.920
you needed to take one
of the earlier flights.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.790
Then you would work there.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.320
You would fly back

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.470
to your office location
on Thursday evening.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.340
And in this time period, usually
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.150
it was, I'd say, the average
to work until midnight or so,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.430
often, of course with breaks at lunch

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.050
and for dinner and so on.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:10.980
So in these days,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.250
if you were able to close
your laptop at 10:00 p.m.,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.020
that would've been a great day.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.360
Thursday was the day where
you were flying home.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.740
There often you arrived home

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.930
between eight and 10:00 p.m. or so.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.610
And here indeed, then afterwards,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.100
usually there wasn't much
work to be done anymore.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.950
So Thursday was already a bit more relaxed

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.030
in terms of working hours.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.240
And then, Friday usually
was a quite regular day

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.060
often until 5:00 p.m. or 6:00 p.m.,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.040
and then, people were calling it a day.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.050
And this is all something quite important.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.170
There was a big culture of
protecting the weekends,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.030
of avoiding weekend work.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.910
Surely, if there was a very
important business need,

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.860
sometimes you were asked to do some work

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.693
on the weekend as well,

00:04:50.693 --> 00:04:53.640
but this was definitely
just the exception.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.770
If by the way, you are
interested to learn more

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.380
about how such a typical
work week in the life

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.960
of a consultant at McKinsey,
BCG, Bain looks like,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.610
I will insert a video here above

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.920
where I've discussed this
in detail in the past.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:05.790
So now, the question is,

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:08.340
how did this change after the pandemic?

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.686
Now, we'll talk more about home office

00:05:09.686 --> 00:05:13.650
and travel in a moment, but
just in terms of working hours,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.420
also when I asked you,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.820
the consensus seem to be that overall,

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.200
nothing really changed.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.470
So the hours are more or less comparable.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.870
Same thing, usually Monday to Wednesday

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.030
until midnight or so is normal.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.710
10:00 p.m. would be a bit early,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.300
after midnight would be late.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.190
Then, Friday being a bit of a slower day,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.200
and weekends still being free.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.880
Now, there are some nuances to this.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:36.713
Some people say,

00:05:36.713 --> 00:05:38.490
"Well, actually got a little bit worse,"

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.480
because for instance,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.180
the Thursday where people used to travel

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.820
and where this used to be a
bit of a slower day as well,

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.890
now it's just a regular work day

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.680
for all the people who just
work from the home office.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.320
So actually, this added to the hours.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.887
But then, on the other
hand, some people say,

00:05:53.887 --> 00:05:56.197
"Well, on the other
hand side, the Mondays,

00:05:56.197 --> 00:05:57.967
"there you don't need to wake up,

00:05:57.967 --> 00:06:00.547
"stand up that early because
you don't need to catch

00:06:00.547 --> 00:06:03.597
"a flight that leaves at
6:00 a.m. or 7:00 a.m.,

00:06:03.597 --> 00:06:06.450
"so you can sleep in a
bit longer on Mondays."

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.450
So this seems to be more
or less balancing out.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:12.030
Of course, I'd be interested
to hear your opinions as well.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.500
Leave a comment if you've had

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.750
very different experiences than that.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.810
So what is my reflection
on these working hours?

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.060
And of course, everyone is different.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.550
The effect that hours like
this will have on people

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:24.900
might differ quite significantly.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.380
So let me just share my
personal humble experience.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:29.213
And I must say,

00:06:29.213 --> 00:06:31.650
I think the most important
thing that you just need to have

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.080
in your mind as a mindset
when joining consulting

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.120
is that during the week,

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.437
you focus 100% on your work.

00:06:39.437 --> 00:06:42.720
So you just should not
have the expectation

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.930
that in the evenings, you
have time to do other things,

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.560
meet with friends, relax
with a Netflix movie,

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.420
follow, pursue a hobby.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.940
This would just not be
part of the reality,

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.350
and you need to be okay with that.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:58.410
And for me, a joint consultant
fresh out of university,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.450
I never really experienced it differently

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.040
after starting working.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.570
So for me, it was just natural.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.290
It did feel like I adapted
to this situation quite well.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.940
And then, of course the good thing,

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.920
which I think also
distinguishes consulting

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.800
from other professions
such as investment banking,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.600
was that the weekends were free.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.270
So from Friday afternoon, Friday evening,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.300
you had some free time to
really recharge the batteries

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.010
and keep up with your social life.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.770
That being said, the hours
can be brutal though.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.380
They differ also significantly by project.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.120
So sometimes you have a tougher project,

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.740
sometimes an easier project.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.690
But especially on the tougher projects,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.020
I remember several weeks,
sometimes even months,

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.580
where I just worked so much
that I just felt so exhausted.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.100
And for sure this would
not have been healthy

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.590
for me to continue working
in such an environment now

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.750
for years, and years, and years.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.700
Surely everyone is different, but for me,

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.680
it was clear that at some point

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.140
I did want to make the transition,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:59.100
make the switch to a more also
family-friendly environment.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.230
Let's talk about travel
and working from home

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.270
before and after the pandemic.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.460
And as I just shared, before the pandemic,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.650
the schedule looked
like Monday to Thursday,

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.660
you would be usually co-located

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.180
with the client at the client location,

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.520
unless the project
location was your hometown,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.330
this could mean significant
travel every single week.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.150
And then, on Friday, at least
the way I experienced it,

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.600
it was absolutely okay to just
work from the home office.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.550
The only exception being
that if your project lead

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.050
was from your office as well,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.480
he or she might have asked you to join

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.820
him or her in the office,
and then, of course,

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.100
home office was not
possible on Fridays anymore.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.350
So what does the
situation look like today?

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.880
And what you have shared with me

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.830
via the surveys is that actually,

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.850
there's a very significant
variance even within the firm.

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.560
And this depends on, first,

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.780
your client needs and preferences.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.030
What actually the client wants you to do.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:54.090
Second, the preferences of your team lead,

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.710
and also of your partners.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.210
And third, to some extent,

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.460
you might even be able also as a junior

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.650
to influence these types of decisions.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.960
And currently there are three archetypes

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.510
of client service models
that are emerging.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.730
The first archetype is the old model

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.040
where consulting teams
are still flying in,

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.260
will spend Monday to Thursday

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.690
with the client on their project.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.090
This does not seem to be the norm anymore,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.390
but it's still used if
the client requests this,

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.070
if the client wants this,

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.380
or for whatever other reason
the partners discussed

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.210
with the client that this is the setup

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.010
that makes sense for the specific project.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:30.510
The second archetype seems to be one

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:33.060
that seems to be especially
relevant for the MBB firms,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.200
McKinsey, BCG, Bain.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.570
And this is that you're
not necessarily co-located

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.010
with your client anymore.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.220
Often also, the client
organizations work remote,

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.630
also from the client side,

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.970
or the employees won't
be in the office anymore.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.580
But instead, just the team internally

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.670
decides to co-locate
in any of the offices.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.260
And what this means is that then often,

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.900
office is being chose at the
beginning of the project.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.210
And then, all team members
who are not originally

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:01.050
from that office need to come
to this office every week.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:02.940
So this means that the
team members that are based

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.940
in other offices need to
fly in Monday morning,

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:09.180
will spend the whole work week
with the team in the office.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.380
And then, Thursday evening,

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.290
they can fly out to
their home office again,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.660
and then, spend the Friday
in the home office indeed.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:16.493
Now of course,

00:10:16.493 --> 00:10:18.000
the office location will often be chosen

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.950
based on the locations
of the team members.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.410
So many team members
already based in one office,

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.450
then likely this is going to be the office

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.250
where everyone will be working from.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.100
So for a part of the team,
this might be quite convenient.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:29.933
But of course,

00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:33.150
this means that all the other
people still need to fly in,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.010
have all the travel that is going on.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.300
At least for this part of the team,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.640
the travel situation did not change much

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.340
before the pandemic, but of
course, project-by-project,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.740
this will likely be
mixed up, so on average,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.750
you should be better off if your goal,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.030
your preference is to
not travel that much.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:49.740
Now to the third archetype,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.450
and this is the fully remote,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.280
or at least hybrid model,

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:55.620
where people can decide
to spend the whole week

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:56.940
from the home office,

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.360
or at least a certain number
of days from the home office

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.910
instead of being co-located
in your own office.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:05.610
This seems to be more
common for Big Four firms

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.680
what you shared with me,
but some project teams,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.080
also the MBB firms follow a similar model.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.940
The what is the implication of this?

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.250
Does a higher number of home office days

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.710
actually improve work-life balance?

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.530
And here actually, I got
quite mixed feedback.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.097
So on the one hand side, I heard that,

00:11:21.097 --> 00:11:23.437
"Yes, this does lead
to a better lifestyle.

00:11:23.437 --> 00:11:25.860
"You have a bit more
flexibility throughout the day."

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:26.880
Surely, if you have family,

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.820
you might have the opportunity
to have lunch with your kids,

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.470
maybe with your significant other.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:34.560
But there's also quite big
group who told me that actually,

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.490
they perceive the work-life
balance to have worsened

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:40.590
due to this for the reason that now,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.470
all this time that you
usually spend in travel,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:44.880
also Thursday evenings

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.310
where you were just at the
airport in the airplane,

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:51.240
this was usually time where
you were able to wind down,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.630
to just relax a bit, maybe
think about some other things,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.130
not think about work all day.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.810
And then, again, once you arrived home,

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.000
you had more time for yourselves.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.590
Nowadays, if you work from home,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.200
you can expect to be
dialed into video calls

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.680
from very early morning
until very late at night

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.440
with very little breaks in between.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:14.760
And this in itself is a new
level of just mental stress,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.260
pressure on your mental health.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.400
It wasn't there before.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:18.990
We were just sitting in the team room

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.850
having eye-to-eye conversations,

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.450
spending time maybe at the
coffee machine with the client.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.120
These are types of
interruptions and breaks

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.370
which are far less common today.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.500
In addition to that, before the pandemic,

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:34.110
video calls weren't really a thing.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.300
Yes, you often had conference calls,

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.770
but these were usually telephone
conferences, so voice only.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.860
And this then adds to just an
additional level of stress.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.900
We need to be sitting
in front of the camera

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.270
managing your looks and appearance.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.670
You cannot just stand
up and do something else

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.950
while being dialed into
a telephone conference.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.070
And all of this just
adds an additional layer

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.350
of stress to the reality of work.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.330
And I can personally totally relate

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:01.740
where I absolutely understand

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:04.080
the advantages of video conferences.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:06.720
It is surely much more
comfortable and convenient

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:10.290
to just talk via voice
without an image of yourself

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:11.910
visibile at every single point in time.

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.920
So let's now move to the last section,

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.890
and this is my reflection of
what all of this will mean

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.260
for the attractiveness
of a consulting career.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.990
And I'd say there are some
positives and some negatives.

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:23.610
Let's start with the positives.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.443
First of all,

00:13:24.443 --> 00:13:27.810
the work from home model adds
flexibility to your schedule.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:29.970
You will have more time with your family,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.620
potentially with your kids.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.570
I do think that this is a positive step

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.750
towards making the consulting
career more family-friendly.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.520
In addition, you will
have some flexibility

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:42.180
in the staffing process to
actively choose for projects,

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.910
for studies that meet your preferences.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:49.050
Also when it comes to being
co-located to the working model,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.080
all of this gives you
agency and helps you select

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.150
the projects that best meet your needs.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.830
But there are some negatives as well,

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:58.080
and for the reasons discussed earlier,

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.650
I'm a bit worried that working
from home in consulting

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.040
will lead to even more
mental health issues,

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.230
to even more pressure on the people.

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:08.430
Sitting in front of the screen

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.890
from early morning until late at night

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.140
being dialed into one video conference

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.650
after video conference is
surely not the healthiest way

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:17.490
to spend your day.

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:19.530
Second, I'm afraid that
the increasing trend

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.810
of working from home takes away one

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:25.140
of the most significant
learning opportunities

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.660
that associated to working in consulting,

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:30.240
and these are regular client interactions.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.700
So in the old model, you
spend Monday to Thursday,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.340
almost the full week
together with your clients.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.680
You will sit together with
them every single day,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.810
have informal conversations with them,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.040
good relationships with them.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.750
And in my humble opinion,

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:46.500
this was one of the most
significant learning aspects

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.420
of the career in consulting.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.070
And sure, if you work from home,

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.440
you will still have video
calls with these people,

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.150
but it's just not the same thing.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.070
And I know some people might disagree,

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:57.720
but also looking at my current role

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.390
as an executive in a startup company,

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:03.180
if you talk to clients
just via video calls

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.310
and compare those to clients you have met

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:07.770
face-to-face in workshops and so on,

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:11.430
the difference of the
relationship is so significant.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:14.610
I am afraid that a lot of
these types of experiences

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:17.040
and learning opportunities
are now missing.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:17.873
While in this video,

00:15:17.873 --> 00:15:19.830
we talked a lot about the negative sides

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:21.000
of working in consulting,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.610
I do want to stress that
I look back at my time

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:26.400
in consulting in a very,
very favorable way.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.610
My time in consulting was
absolutely instrumental

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.760
to both my personal and my
professional development.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.110
Yes, the hours can be tough,

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.930
but you will also grow
significantly with the experience.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.030
Now, if you are currently
working in consulting as well,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:40.200
let me know in the comments

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:43.110
whether all of this matches
your current experiences.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.850
And of course, if you took any value

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:45.990
out of this video at all,

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:49.290
hit the like button for the
YouTube algorithm and subscribe.

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.200
Big, big thanks to all the
members of the channel as well.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.310
My name is Heinrich.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.230
I release videos every single week.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.063
All the best to you, and bye-bye.

